{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790535538,"nonce":"9LieBCedJEgAV8pwnXtAIKBiDQY=","title":"Web window: a frame that requests its redraw after presenting costs an extra vsync","message":"In a page, a loop that handles `RedrawRequested` by rendering and then calling `window.request_redraw()` drops to about 40 fps once rendering takes a sizeable part of the frame, though the work fits in one.\n\nMeasured in examples/browser-interop at 2000 birds (Chrome, desktop): the Wren step takes 8-9 ms and the per-bird reads 0.4 ms, yet 300 frames took 7.5 s. Moving `request_redraw()` before `render()` gives 300 frames in 4.9 s (60 fps) with the same work. With a light frame (200 birds) either order holds 60 fps.\n\nLikely cause: `presentSurface` returns only after the GPU agent flushes the frame at its next animation frame (`wire.after`), so a redraw requested after it is queued for the animation frame after that. Natively the order does not matter. Either present should not hold the program past the flush, or the window's web backend should count a redraw requested during a frame's handling toward the next animation frame. Both examples now request first.","files":null}]}